WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.939
Film Courage: Harvey is every day a work day
for you?

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:04.180
Harvey Mason, Jr: Simple answer, yes.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.180
Every day is a work day.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.339
Yeah, there is a lot to do.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:12.100
If I’m not at the studio, I’m on the phone,
emailing, doing a ton of meetings.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.420
There’s a lot of discussion, talking about
what is coming up next, talking about the

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.070
song we are working on, talking about the
scenes.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.390
It’s always work.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:25.400
Film Courage: What time do you normally start
your day and do you put in eight hours?

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.300
Harvey Mason, Jr: …I put in more like 18
hours.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:38.660
I start anywhere from 9 or 10 in the morning
until 10, 11, 12 at night.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.050
Sometimes into the early morning, if I’m
working on a project.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.840
It just really depends on where I am in the
phase of the project.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.510
Film Courage: You always hear about entrepreneurs
that they can’t really ‘turn it off.’

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.670
They are always working.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:54.650
Whether they are at a restaurant and they
are on “vacation.”

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.250
How do you deal with this, as part of your
life?

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.210
Harvey Mason, Jr: Yeah, I do suffer from that
a little bit.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.220
As I said I have kids.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.850
So that pulls me out of it, for sure.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.469
When I’m around my kids I’m not really
thinking about work.

00:01:06.469 --> 00:01:12.110
But it’s tough, if you love what you do,
you want to continue to get better.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.399
You want to continue to grow.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:14.399
You want to build.

00:01:14.399 --> 00:01:15.999
At least I do.

00:01:15.999 --> 00:01:16.999
And I love making music.

00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:19.859
I love working with the people that I work
with.

00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:20.920
I’m excited.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.319
It’s not work to me, it’s fun.

00:01:23.319 --> 00:01:26.979
Film Courage: How satisfied are you with your
work at the end of the day?

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:30.039
Or are there times where you’re not satisfied
and that’s almost a good thing because it

00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:31.329
keeps pushing you?

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:35.969
Harvey Mason, Jr: No…I think there are times
when I’m satisfied and I try not to stop

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:37.789
a project until I am satisfied.

00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:42.880
I don’t do things that I don’t like and
I don’t turn in things that I’m not proud

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:43.880
of.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.840
When I’m finished with a project, I’m
usually pretty satisfied.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.969
I think production is a series of compromises
sometimes because you can’t always get everything

00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:55.899
exactly the way you want.

00:01:55.899 --> 00:01:57.399
Maybe that is the perfectionist in me?

00:01:57.399 --> 00:01:58.539
But I’m always tinkering.

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:01.100
I’m always trying to do something else.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.100
Let me try this tempo.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.770
Let me try to add this instrument or take
this background vocal out or build this bigger.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:08.770
But that’s the fun of it.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:14.250
It’s like building a skyscraper and doing
anything you want.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.930
Just adding another floor and adding some
more windows.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.760
And just continuing to build this amazing
kind of (hopefully) masterpiece.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.319
And at the end of it, if you’re not happy
and you’re not proud, you did something

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:27.319
wrong.

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:28.810
You probably better go back and rework it.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.269
So, I get a lot of satisfaction from my finished
projects.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:36.230
Film Courage: Was [a strong] work ethic something
you were born with or something over time

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.750
you picked up, whether it’s the sportsman
in you…

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.870
Harvey Mason, Jr: I think it’s a combination.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:48.170
I definitely was into working hard but I learned
a lot from my Dad who was really, really a

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.180
hard worker.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.090
He preached that at home a lot.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:57.190
Dedicating yourself and sacrificing and making
the most of your talents.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:02.400
My Mom and my Dad were both musicians so they
were all about practicing and getting better.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.480
And then I had some great coaches.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.400
I had an amazing high school coach, Coach
John Giuffrida and a college Coach Lute Olson

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.870
who is in the basketball hall of fame and
those guys both showed me the meaning of setting

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:20.010
a high bar, setting goals, accomplishing things
you don’t think you can accomplish, expecting

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.610
more from yourself than you thought you could
do.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.299
And that definitely translated to music.

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:28.530
And I think that is something a lot of musicians
don’t have because they didn’t play sports

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.530
like I did.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.790
I played sports my whole life.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.859
Since I was 7 years old I was playing sports,
all the way through college.

00:03:35.859 --> 00:03:41.040
And playing at college at that high, high
level you have to be hyper-competitive and

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:49.210
you have to be sacrificing and intense and
goal-oriented and all the things you have

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:54.049
to do to play high-level college basketball
or professional basketball.

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:59.049
Those aren't things that most musicians do,
so I brought that to this end to what I do.

00:03:59.049 --> 00:04:00.049
I don’t know.

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:01.270
I think it’s worked for me.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.909
Hopefully I haven’t made too many people
angry how competitive I am.

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:05.150
But I think it usually works.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.219
Film Courage: I heard a quote one time that
said ‘If people in Hollywood aren’t mad

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:09.840
at you, you aren’t doing anything right
[laughs].’

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.310
Harvey Mason, Jr: Yeah…I guess so.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:12.480
I don’t know.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.700
I’ve tried to be easy to work with.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.560
I’ve tried to be the person that people
enjoy being around.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.850
I think at a certain level, a lot of the CEOs
of these huge companies, that’s when it

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:28.449
gets to the point where people are going to
be mad at you one way or another.

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:29.960
Right now I feel like I’m in a good place.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.699
I feel like my company is in a good place,
people respect what we do.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:36.060
They find it easy to work with us.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.600
I think it’s still a service business at
some point.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.040
You are providing someone with a service so
I think it’s important that you treat people

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.960
the right way and I don’t know…hopefully
people like me.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:51.430
Film Courage: I actually meant it in a way
that sometimes people in this town resent

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:54.190
people for getting a project they didn’t
get or something like that.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.550
Harvey Mason, Jr: Yeah, I think that’s true.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.370
There is definitely some of that.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:58.430
I don’t do that.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:04.139
I think there is so much great work that anytime
somebody else gets something, I think it’s

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:05.139
great for them.

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:10.170
And it’s not like I’m dying to have all
those other peoples’ projects.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:16.650
I try to stay positive and be thankful for
what I have and I try and support other people

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.789
and encourage them as well.

00:05:18.789 --> 00:05:21.099
So we need more of that in Los Angeles.

00:05:21.099 --> 00:05:22.099
Film Courage: We do.

00:05:22.099 --> 00:05:23.099
We do.

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:27.729
I saw an infographic that talked about entrepreneurs
and it said that (some of the traits were)

00:05:27.729 --> 00:05:32.740
that they were 1) obsessive 2) that they kind
of had ideas which others couldn’t understand.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:35.880
That’s what set them apart from other people.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.580
Do you think the people that come here [to
Los Angeles] maybe aren’t obsessive enough

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.620
or competitive enough and then they leave
and go back home because they have friends

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.830
and family waiting for them?

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:49.660
Whereas if you have that sportsman’s mentality,
where you almost have to win, that is going

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:50.660
to set you apart?

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.870
Harvey Mason, Jr: Yeah…I think that is something
that sets people apart.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:58.030
I think that anyone who has accomplished anything
really, really amazing…I mean there are

00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:59.970
a few that are lucky, but most are not lucky.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:06.250
It’s just really hard work and it’s competitive
or being intense and sacrificing and that’s

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.800
really in any craft or trait.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.770
I think if you look at any of the biggest
actors, they’re not the biggest actors for

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:12.770
no reason.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:14.392
I worked with a couple of them on this film
[SING Movie].

00:06:14.392 --> 00:06:17.160
Reese Witherspoon is a powerhouse.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.039
She produces, directs, she acts, she sings,
she does everything.

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:23.780
And she’s that successful because 1).

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.009
She’s smart and really talented but there
are thousands of smart, talented people.

00:06:28.009 --> 00:06:34.110
She is driven and maybe I’m overstepping
my bounds because I’m not that close with

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.110
her.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:41.500
But from what I have observed, she’s super
hard-working, super smart, super focused and

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.820
I’m sure, somewhat competitive.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:44.820
She wants to win.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.174
So I think that is something that sets you
apart.

00:06:46.174 --> 00:06:46.190
Question for the Viewers: Do you like to work
more than 8 hours a day?

